Stop the daemon's output device from blocking its own capture side

Field evidence (fuser -v /dev/snd/*) from the UMC1820 install showed the
daemon itself holding the card's capture node (pcmC2D0c) open alongside its
active playback stream - while reporting "Input device not found" for that
same card's input. PipeWire was only attached to a different card.

Root cause: cpal's ALSA backend opens a device for BOTH directions when it
is resolved and keeps whichever handles succeeded inside the Device struct;
building a stream takes only the playback handle out. main() resolved the
output device into a binding that lived for the life of the process, so the
leftover idle capture handle held the card's single hw capture substream
forever. Input enumeration (which only lists devices it can open for
capture at that moment) then couldn't see the card at all - the daemon was
EBUSY-blocking itself, and also blocking every other capture application
(arecord, dsnoop, a second listing run) for as long as it ran.

Fix in main(): drop the resolved output device before inputs are opened,
and re-resolve it only at the moment the output stream is built, dropping
it again immediately after. cpal's StreamInner keeps only the playback PCM
it takes, so nothing retains the capture side. The input path was checked
for the mirror problem: its device is a function local that drops on
return, and no struct in the codebase retains a cpal Device.

Verification: clean release build with zero warnings and the full test
suite green (503 lib + 28 http-api + 1 broker-restart + 26 streaming).
The failure itself needs real audio hardware with a single-substream
capture device, which this development environment does not have, so the
end-to-end confirmation is the field install restarting on this build.

Prompts that led to this work:

- "Are we sure this isn't an issue caused by the app opening the card for
  output and then ALSA considering that the handle and not letting us open
